.TH CLIPBOARD-MANAGER 1 "2026-10-08" "clipboard-manager 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
clipboard-manager \- clipboard history for Linux desktops
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B clipboard-manager
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B clipboard-manager
keeps a searchable history of everything you copy, in the style of the
Windows 10/11 clipboard flyout. Run with no command it becomes the background
service for your session; run with a command it sends that command to the
running service and exits.
.PP
Press
.B Ctrl+`
(Ctrl together with the backtick/grave key above Tab) to open a compact panel
near the pointer. Type to filter, use the arrow keys to move, and press Return
to put an entry back on the clipboard. The panel then closes and you paste with
your own paste key.
.PP
Selecting an entry
.B only
places it on the clipboard. No keystrokes are injected into other
applications.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B toggle
Open the panel if it is closed, close it if it is open. This is the command to
bind to a key if your desktop cannot register the shortcut automatically.
.TP
.B show
Open the panel.
.TP
.B hide
Close the panel.
.TP
.BR clear " [" \-\-include\-pinned ]
Delete history. Pinned entries are kept unless
.B \-\-include\-pinned
is given.
.TP
.B pause
Stop recording new entries. Existing history remains readable.
.TP
.B resume
Start recording again.
.TP
.B status
Print the session type, the monitoring backend, whether the keyboard shortcut
is actually registered, and how much history is stored. Exits non-zero if the
shortcut is
.B not
working, so it is usable in a script.
.TP
.BI search " QUERY"
Print matching entries, one per line, as tab-separated
.IR id ,
.IR type ,
.I age
and preview.
.TP
.B settings
Open the settings window.
.TP
.B quit
Shut the background service down.
.TP
.BI export " FILE"
Write the history to a JSON file.
.B This file contains your clipboard history in readable form, including any
.B passwords or tokens you have copied.
Refuses to run while the service holds the database; use Settings → Advanced,
or stop the service first.
.TP
.BI import " FILE"
Merge a previously exported file into the history.
.TP
.B install
Install the systemd user service and autostart entry for the current user, and
try to register the keyboard shortcut. Do
.B not
run this with sudo.
.TP
.B uninstall
Remove them again. Clipboard history is left in place.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Log at debug level. Clipboard contents are never logged, at any level.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH KEYBOARD
Inside the panel:
.TP
.B Ctrl+F
Focus the search field.
.TP
.BR Up / Down
Move between entries.
.TP
.B Return
Put the selected entry on the clipboard.
.TP
.B Delete
Delete the selected entry.
.TP
.B Ctrl+P
Pin or unpin the selected entry.
.TP
.B Escape
Close the panel.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/clipboard-manager/config.toml
Settings. Written by the settings window; safe to edit by hand.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/clipboard-manager/database.sqlite
The history database. Mode 0600 in a 0700 directory.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/clipboard-manager/blobs/
Content-addressed storage for images and other large payloads.
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/clipboard-manager.service
The user service, written by
.BR "clipboard-manager install" .
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/clipboard-manager/
The per-user command socket and instance lock. Not reachable by other users.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY\fR, \fBDISPLAY
Used to detect the session type and choose a monitoring backend.
.TP
.B XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP
Used only to pick the shortcut mechanism and tray behaviour.
.SH PLATFORM NOTES
On
.B X11
the shortcut is grabbed directly from the X server and the source application
of a copy can be identified, so the exclusion list works.
.PP
On
.B Wayland
an application cannot grab a key or read the clipboard without a protocol for
it. Where the compositor provides
.I zwlr_data_control_manager_v1
(KDE Plasma 5.25 and later, Sway, Hyprland, Wayfire, river) that is used. The
protocol carries no application identity, so the exclusion list has
.B no effect
there; the password-manager hint still works. On
.B GNOME
Wayland, where mutter provides no such protocol, history is read from the X11
clipboard through Xwayland, which mutter keeps synchronised in both directions.
This needs no configuration change and does not require running the desktop
under Xwayland.
.PP
Run
.B clipboard-manager status
to see which backend is in use and any limitation that applies.
.SH PRIVACY
Clipboard data is never transmitted, uploaded or sent to telemetry, and the
application works entirely offline. The systemd unit sets
.I PrivateNetwork=true
and
.IR IPAddressDeny=any .
Clipboard contents are never written to the log.
.PP
Entries that a password manager marks as a password are skipped. Under X11,
applications can additionally be excluded by name in Settings → Privacy.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The command failed, no service is running, or
.B status
found the keyboard shortcut not registered.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR systemctl (1)
.PP
Logs:
.B journalctl \-\-user \-u clipboard-manager
.SH BUGS
https://github.com/sridharreddy23/clipboard-manager/issues
.SH AUTHOR
sridhar
